esents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33af366f-8b9d-57b5-8b8e-e61fbf203c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352047} {:answer/id #uuid "811b20a6-b4e9-56ab-9c01-7e145c7ec18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352691} {:answer/id #uuid "b227f1ed-1303-5d83-98ed-934929ab4d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353088} {:answer/id #uuid "be6b0c87-8a6f-5884-91ad-766311701780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353195} {:answer/id #uuid "e222d1da-c9ae-5955-8ccf-d35550ba041f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b227f1ed-1303-5d83-98ed-934929ab4d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353088} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f597768-c079-5381-9b03-92aa2ab1d070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f92163d-ae56-5dad-9ed9-3681dc9f9768" :question/math-academy-id "q-85177" :question/problem "![](images/97/97dfdde016e4c3482fe036379e22aa95af27c1bcf93076ea9054e263100ad8e1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c689e941-40af-5da5-a7a6-8f097d337bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{-\\pi /2}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394656} {:answer/id #uuid "e1111694-d4c6-57dd-aac5-03ecc11cd461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394657} {:answer/id #uuid "c05d30b4-542f-5261-99b1-b4d7b3e9726f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{9}^{25}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394658} {:answer/id #uuid "ae89d88e-1628-56b0-b3ba-6c1c89aca0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394659} {:answer/id #uuid "227ccf05-1a74-595f-94d5-213b809441cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{9}^{25}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae89d88e-1628-56b0-b3ba-6c1c89aca0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394659} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53e1dc7-e5ea-5878-8585-ae4e73e89cea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cfa5c22-7854-5e49-ad77-545303651a0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85061" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec2b27e99ad03df281464b1a1fb08b5e8cbf869acf1892a815d9031bf506dafe.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the third quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21488b88-2acf-5a8b-8507-da4310f1130f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424300} {:answer/id #uuid "66e1a5e9-95e1-574c-a839-41136951a82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424308} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4c807c-3751-5c8a-ba9f-d367e6a9e011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424316} {:answer/id #uuid "e203c470-87b8-5386-8b7b-5e7971c12cc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424318} {:answer/id #uuid "f154bcd4-8668-5f01-afce-656a9407bcc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac4c807c-3751-5c8a-ba9f-d367e6a9e011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a78d9d7e-8041-59b2-a1b7-9e4905f4e6d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424324}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0b5051e-fba0-51c4-bd0d-e4424f08ff88" :question/math-academy-id "q-85175" :question/problem "![](images/44/44b6b5826b5d8532fef3a9c8e56a03b3244a4aaa33ae00b4b12b69364c51f001.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the right-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24cfcda8-afbc-5331-885b-bcfa05c0d741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424301} {:answer/id #uuid "5553ca7e-bc04-53a9-ab03-d33a73524410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424307} {:answer/id #uuid "778dd242-bc8c-5163-ba5e-b482fc9a6724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424310} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdc8ff4-a08f-5057-bc05-25918e661b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424314} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4cbdd3-75cd-5bc5-8f98-2159597bf317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5553ca7e-bc04-53a9-ab03-d33a73524410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424307} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf8a023-f586-55e1-889c-e57ed171c509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424325}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff6bf6f1-3574-59b2-8046-e358528c7f1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83946" :question/problem "![](images/31/31054ef4614814dd1c9131192504ea37f3527af75d8292fbce943c16235c9303.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A$ \nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157170 :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048157 :topic/math-academy-id 394 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations Using a Difference of Squares"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774 :topic/math-academy-id 375 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations by Factoring"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aef340c0-cff5-5bfa-b9a1-2e0d4a2e3f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef340c0-cff5-5bfa-b9a1-2e0d4a2e3f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289190} :answer-field/id #uuid "ada5e9bd-53a7-576a-8573-bd6cafce876c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6fbb46-cecc-525a-94f8-20e139099f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6fbb46-cecc-525a-94f8-20e139099f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288993} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5397a8-9380-5e98-988d-5860f188cfc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3ec1edd-6bd3-59bd-aa3e-85aa30d0d59b" :question/math-academy-id "q-237947" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+5x\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {4}^{x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+5x\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {4}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x+6){4}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x+3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,-2.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157958 :q