37287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea3ba58b-f375-5901-a123-b25fc65695af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237287} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e46ebc-53b2-5521-88ed-68aaf1f4f829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19bca5be-bffd-5da5-b2f8-88bf9c5fec74" :question/math-academy-id "q-100781" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8x+12){7}^{x-3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x-3}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x-3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=2,6,$ and their product is $2\\cdot 6=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b49158d-1e9b-5620-aa68-4bc86800e77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235985} {:answer/id #uuid "402eb34a-619c-5c28-b06d-a763be8a0260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236227} {:answer/id #uuid "89710b44-592c-5600-8225-1ded09fa8b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236678} {:answer/id #uuid "8cedd7cd-8213-5fa6-a8a1-aab66b50379a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236708} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4daebb-fc52-5b89-9526-438c8a3b6213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b49158d-1e9b-5620-aa68-4bc86800e77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235985} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda41c81-3c6e-51fc-b6bb-6060a77b012b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "731d88c0-04ba-5bfa-af89-56694fda5912" :question/math-academy-id "q-100785" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-36){e}^{9x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-36=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{9x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-36=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+6)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{9x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{9x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-6,6,$ and their product is $(-6)\\cdot 6=-36.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b298d90-e2b4-5471-976c-b8ed9690b02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b298d90-e2b4-5471-976c-b8ed9690b02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288204} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf610bb3-bb99-5ba5-937a-a2246750dd7d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d10c43-57f4-5ac0-9f7d-8bebd2d13863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d10c43-57f4-5ac0-9f7d-8bebd2d13863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebecd072-9bc9-56ba-be2a-35c69511a3f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290222}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa483285-7fb1-55f0-9e07-a601fc5a4d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-237946" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-9)\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-9=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,3.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c3f287-5dae-5601-8ebb-1ede42e5ca46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c3f287-5dae-5601-8ebb-1ede42e5ca46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46a4763-9986-5fe1-adec-09a176e52d03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7541a123-c295-5764-92f6-cc633d5666e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7541a123-c295-5764-92f6-cc633d5666e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291133} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77cc2d2-1e8e-508d-83cc-6c1e4f0ee7f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292392}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6bdd7ef-3aa2-50b8-bd67-7fd5c008275b" :question/math-academy-id "q-264754" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)(x+5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d579f97-0ec1-5880-be03-82e505ce65d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d579f97-0ec1-5880-be03-82e505ce65d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290753} :answer-field/id #uuid "accc6009-df01-5cf7-b489-f8abf6c8772b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292273} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8c0005f-1c7c-58e1-8ace-35c2f623dd50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8c0005f-1c7c-58e1-8ace-35c2f623dd50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291682} :answer-field/id #uuid "daaa6d02-1f99-53bf-abf0-10dac7839c78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f62fb8d-cfd4-5a86-8152-bac3c04d2838" :question/math-academy-id "q-264772" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+4x-48){3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+2x-24) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+6)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77c3cd4-4822-5b88-b531-15ff6b1e643c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e319a330-863d-5a2b-83b4-4b5600e073c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292441}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77ac52fb-b13e-5bb0-9619-5d3b00902a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-264778" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a78efb5-52b6-5d3c-b5a0-49f39f6da8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a78efb5-52b6-5d3c-b5a0-49f39f6da8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291022} :answer-field/id #uuid "818baac4-9846-582e-a016-d7c37e9a7e42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37e3585-e3ba-5017-8c94-4dbab9c04a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a37e3585-e3ba-5017-8c94-4dbab9c04a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291345} :answer-field/id #uuid "885f1080-4f56-5ac6-a8d7-1a1b1f221f7d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f911a188-0d00-5124-b074-90cbb871a900" :question/math-academy-id "q-264780" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+11x+28){5}^{2x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x+28=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{2x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+11x+28=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+7)(x+4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-7,-4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{2x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{2x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-7\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292ef02d-b7cd-5a35-82b0-cbb44129f1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351949} {:answer/id #uuid "871be151-8a04-536c-bbbf-3c044fc1b69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352749} {:answer/id #uuid "9121608d-7af0-59ef-b803-1e106a41ad4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352825} {:answer/id #uuid "e34b0cee-72c8-5bf5-989a-5a931ef5ee95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353547} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fb693c-d101-5072-82da-c04f62a50942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34b0cee-72c8-5bf5-989a-5a931ef5ee95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353547} :answer-field/id #uuid "659dbad8-622b-5bc5-99b1-3e39b5187855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354004}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ebf2795-4a5b-5fc4-bae2-4108180a784e" :question/math-academy-id "q-88458" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-7x+12){3}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-7x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=3,4.$\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=3,4,$ and their product is $3\\cdot 4=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33737a4b-5342-5565-914a-cb6438076b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352043} {:answer/id #uuid "380810d5-4536-54a8-96a6-03ca61571cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352085} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84059a-bf3e-5ad8-848d-de47fb5fcbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352275} {:answer/id #uuid "8597e99b-a624-5fb1-9f86-e86812b0613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352731} {:answer/id #uuid "da180e83-5bee-502b-9c12-ae8aed810433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da180e83-5bee-502b-9c12-ae8aed810433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a5b309-fce0-58d4-9cb6-f4a8f8750bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "395668d0-2b32-5884-a30f-d67a196224fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88460" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "745439dc-de09-51c8-bbdb-eedea4d0f155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386573} {:answer/id #uuid "617f1c2d-99ac-5b8a-931f-683ccf1893b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386574} {:answer/id #uuid "db8e533b-8461-5017-a4f6-47771a90f0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386575} {:answer/id #uuid "e510aa7f-7425-5853-824c-91e79e70849b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386576} {:answer/id #uuid "2d786935-adc1-5afa-a4cc-25f126fdd263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e510aa7f-7425-5853-824c-91e79e70849b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386576} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a99769-405d-5ab0-ad5f-e1bf8543c410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11afc858-764d-5d9d-a321-3ddf5528f806" :question/math-academy-id "q-110801" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1)\\cdot {7}^{2-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2-x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-1=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-1,1.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2-x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2-x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-1,1,$ and their product is $-1\\cdot 1=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34bd077-9be1-5cd3-be13-0e0181077e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394774} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5c3e71-7f6d-594f-a826-d9fafbec86ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394775} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd7941b-aaf2-5165-bdc1-54cb7f6a2561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394776} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcbe70-bf08-5ff6-83d7-c6b4c28a448a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394777} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f535b7-4732-53ed-9c80-3f234b68b109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcbe70-bf08-5ff6-83d7-c6b4c28a448a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394777} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a33ee9e-e01b-57c4-b697-df179407774b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394773}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d2284d9-59bd-591b-a5ed-82b600cb5dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-88454" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-25)\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-25=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+5)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,5,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot 5=-25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef56dae1-235e-58a4-9d49-dac58019c11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394781} {:answer/id #uuid "5d542604-cc75-5fe8-a314-152f5e0d0baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394782} {:answer/id #uuid "98750137-8ecd-58a9-830f-28a32bdedfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394783} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e12eec-8e62-581b-b20f-14c8b66d8602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394784} {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16451bc-a0a9-548c-ba07-f9abe1416b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394780}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d7afbe-c052-55bc-910e-6663c48abfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88459" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x+10){2}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+1)(x+5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,-1.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,-1,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}]}
